# Go Testing (DBO)

## Structure

- Colocate `*_test.go` next to implementation.
- Use table-driven tests with `t.Run` subtests.
- Call `t.Parallel()` at test and subtest level.
- Name tests: `Test<FunctionOrBehavior>`.

```go
func TestOverridePostgresqlURIDatabase(t *testing.T) {
    t.Parallel()
    tests := []struct {
        name, uri, database, want string
    }{
        {name: "replaces db name", uri: "postgres://u:p@h/old", database: "new", want: "postgres://u:p@h/new"},
    }
    for _, tt := range tests {
        t.Run(tt.name, func(t *testing.T) {
            t.Parallel()
            got := overridePostgresqlURIDatabase(tt.uri, tt.database)
            if got != tt.want {
                t.Errorf("got %q, want %q", got, tt.want)
            }
        })
    }
}
```

## Database Driver Tests

- Test SQL/command generation without a live DB when possible.
- Construct repo struct directly, assert generated query strings.

## Helpers

- Mark helpers with `t.Helper()`.
- Use `t.Fatalf` for setup failures, `t.Errorf` for assertion failures.

## Commands

```bash
cd backend
go test ./...
go test ./internal/database/... -run TestName
go test ./... -cover
```

## What to Test

- Pure functions, parsers, URI builders, SQL generators — always.
- Service logic — when behavior is non-trivial.
- Handlers — only for complex binding/validation edge cases.
